@@ -0,0 +1,48 @@
+# Correcting a Booked Visit Narrows NG-5
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-5 declined both changing and cancelling a visit once booked. The [confirmation ADR](2026-08-08-non-goal-deletion-and-visit-amendment.md) made both halves deliberate on 2026-07-31 provenance grounds. Its Consequences named the only mechanism for later movement: a recorded owner decision carrying its own non-goal ADR.
+
+The owner made that decision on 2026-08-08 and asked for correction of a booked visit's date and description. Cancellation was not part of the request.
+
+The row's stated reason does not cover both halves equally. Cancellation adds lifecycle state the sample carries nowhere else. Correction does not: the sample already corrects owner and pet details forward, and a corrected visit stores one date and one description exactly as a booked one does.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Leave NG-5 whole and decline the request.** Rejected: the owner decided otherwise, and the row's lifecycle-state reason does not reach correction.
+2. **Retire NG-5 entirely, admitting cancellation too.** Rejected: cancellation still introduces the lifecycle state the row was written to keep out, and it sits next to the deletion NG-4 declines.
+3. **Narrow NG-5 to cancellation alone** (chosen).
+
+## Decision
+
+NG-5 now reads "Cancelling a visit once booked". Correcting a booked visit's date and description is in scope under `REQ-VIS-003`.
+
+Correction is forward-only, holding to booking's rules: the description stays required and the date stays later than today. A correction therefore cannot move a visit into the past, and a past-dated visit moves forward before its description can be fixed.
+
+A correction replaces the date and description of the existing visit. The pet gains no second visit.
+
+The owner's record offers no route to the correction form. The form is reachable only by a reader who already knows the visit's address. A follow-up request may add a visible entry point.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Forward-only correction now covers visits alongside owners and pets. The sample's correction story is uniform across the three.
+- NG-4 is untouched. Deleting an owner, a pet, or a visit stays out of scope.
+- Cancellation stays out. A visit can be re-dated but never withdrawn, so a visit that did not happen has no honest representation.
+- A corrected visit keeps no trace of its previous date or description. The sample records no visit lifecycle state, so corrections are unaudited by construction.
+- Without an entry point on the owner's record, the capability is unreachable by navigation. It exists for a reader who reads the routes.
+- One mutating route is added to an application with no authentication. Any caller who knows a visit's address can correct it. That is the demonstration baseline recorded in [system-design § Security Context](../system-design.md#security-context), not a widening of it: booking is already open to the same caller.
+- Narrowing NG-5 a second time — to admit cancellation — needs its own recorded owner decision. This ADR settles correction only.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-5
+
+- [PRD Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals) — the narrowed row and the preamble naming it decided.
+- [PRD Visits](../prd.md#visits) — `REQ-VIS-003`, its acceptance criteria, and its edge cases.
+
+## References
+
+- [Deleting Records and Amending Booked Visits Are Deliberately Out of Scope](2026-08-08-non-goal-deletion-and-visit-amendment.md) — the confirmation this narrows.
